architecture-audit/cycle-020-app-shell-project-session-browser-boundary/implementation-log.md:146:After rereading `architecture-audit/wave-plan.md`, the current plan ends at Wave 020. Wave 020 resolved the app-shell production composition/browser-default boundary but intentionally left central application-use-case command ownership debt (`SelectionUseCases` direct delete-command construction) and the broader `World`/`CommandStack` application-kernel ownership question. The next action should therefore be a **reviewed wave-plan-extension stage**, not implementation: scout the current source tree and add the next central wave entry that addresses the most important remaining application boundary (likely selection/delete command-use-case ownership or application-kernel/runtime object ownership), with scope, delete list, success criteria, validation requirements, and bounded review before any new cycle starts.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:10:This plan orders waves by architectural centrality, not ease of implementation. The first waves establish the domain/application boundary and authoritative contracts because later persistence, rendering, tooling, UI composition, backend alignment, and documentation waves depend on those decisions. Each wave is intentionally scoped to fit one Phase 3 cycle: scope check, two proposals, dual-reviewer convergence, implementation, validation, preview refresh, and implementation log.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:12:The signed-off baseline ranks the domain/application dependency inversion around `World` as the highest maintainability risk because `World` imports services and command/application classes while commands also import `World` (`architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R1; `architecture-audit/baseline/architecture-map.md`, “Cross-layer leaks and circular/dependency inversions”). The same baseline identifies god-modules, duplicated state paths, persistence/private-cast coupling, UI-to-operation imports, broad `any` contracts, dead legacy workspace files, rendering concentration, disconnected backend validation, and weak architecture tests as the next central risks (`architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R2–R13; `architecture-audit/baseline/quality-report.md`, Q1–Q22).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:22:5. Preserve aggressive deletion: obsolete shims, dead files, placeholder docs, compatibility branches, and duplicate paths must be removed rather than wrapped.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:26:Each implementation wave must run the mandatory validation cadence recorded under that wave’s `validation/` directory: root/workspace lint where defined, focused Vitest for touched modules, full Vitest, root/workspace build where defined, Playwright/e2e for frontend/user-facing/persistence/API changes, and live preview refresh/reachability probe. Waves that touch only documentation in later phases may justify skipping e2e only in their implementation log.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:30:## Wave 001 — Invert the World/application boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:34:- `frontend/src/domain/world/World.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:42:- initialization command/factory paths currently coupled to `World` (`frontend/src/commands/operations/world/InitializationOps.ts`, `frontend/src/commands/factories/ProjectionSetupFactory.ts`, related app startup call sites)
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:47:The baseline states that `World` is the canonical root but imports `DependencyService`, `Command`, initialization factories, process-tree operations, and `CommandStack` while commands import `World`, creating the most central inversion (`risk-register.md` R1; `domain-model.md` “Coupling observations”; `quality-report.md` Q3). It also identifies `Element2D.realize2D(world: World)` and `styleService` walking `World`/`ProcessTree` as domain-internal root coupling (`domain-model.md` corrections; `risk-register.md` R11). Baseline corrections also identify descriptor registration as a runtime composition point: `DescriptorRegistry.ts` maps element classes to descriptor logic and `registerDescriptors.ts` is invoked before app mount, so Wave 001 proposals must evaluate this coupling when removing application/service imports from the domain boundary (`inventory.md` corrections; `architecture-map.md` addendum).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:51:`World` becomes a domain-owned model root with no imports from commands, command stacks, application services, Vue, or persistence. Initialization orchestration lives outside the domain root in an application/use-case layer. Domain realization, descriptor registration, and visibility dependencies are expressed through narrow domain ports/contexts rather than the whole `World` object where practical within this wave. If `styleService.ts` still needs process-tree-specific cleanup after the `World` dependency is removed, that residual ProcessTree ownership question is explicitly deferred to Wave 005 rather than hidden in this boundary wave.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:55:- Delete `World.initialize(commandStack)` or any equivalent domain method that accepts command-stack/application infrastructure.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:56:- Delete domain imports of `Command`, `CommandStack`, command factories, process-tree operation classes, and service-layer dependency helpers from `World.ts`.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:57:- Delete dead compatibility comments or wrappers that preserve the old `World`-drives-commands initialization path.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:58:- Delete tests that assert initialization must be invoked through `World` if they exist; replace with tests for the new application initializer.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:62:- Static imports from `frontend/src/domain/world/World.ts` do not point to `frontend/src/commands/`, `frontend/src/services/`, Vue components, or stores.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:64:- Focused tests cover the new initializer and prove `World` can be constructed as a pure domain object.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:108:Depends on Wave 001 so plane realization and side-effect boundaries are not split around stale `World` application coupling.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:126:The baseline finds that canonical ownership is `World → ProjectionPlane → Element2D`, while `worldStore` separately stores 2D elements, 3D elements, guides, and a dependency graph (`domain-model.md`; `quality-report.md` Q11; `risk-register.md` R3). Corrections classify `Workspace2D.vue` as dead/uncompilable because it imports absent command-class modules, and `ElementRenderer.vue` as dead because it is only reached from `Workspace2D.vue` and depends on legacy store/types (`inventory.md` corrections; `quality-report.md` Q12 correction; `risk-register.md` R7 correction).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:130:The application has one authoritative geometric state source: `World` and its projection planes. Store/composable paths may hold UI state or derived view models, but they no longer own parallel geometry maps. Dead workspace files and legacy structural element paths are removed.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:144:- Snapping/geometry composables either consume `World`/projection-plane read models or are deleted if obsolete.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:158:- `frontend/src/commands/CommandStack.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:161:- `frontend/src/services/ProjectService.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:166:The baseline reports broad `Record<string, any>` operation serialization contracts, `ProjectService` imports of many concrete operations, a monolithic reconstruction switch, and private casts into command/world internals (`quality-report.md` Q6–Q7, Q16; `risk-register.md` R4, R6). It also notes current persistence is command-sourced: committed commands are serialized and deserialization reconstructs operations and replays into a fresh world/stack (`domain-model.md` “Persistence model”).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:175:- Delete schema migration helpers, migration logging, and corrupted-operation compatibility fallbacks in `ProjectService` unless the reviewed proposal proves a current-format-only replacement requires a small typed upgrade step.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:183:- `ProjectService` no longer imports every concrete operation solely to deserialize a project unless a reviewed registry design explicitly centralizes this dependency in a smaller contract module.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:247:The baseline flags UI components directly importing `Command` and concrete operations (`architecture-map.md` leak 3; `risk-register.md` R5; `quality-report.md` Q1, Q4, Q19). The Wave 006 scope check confirmed that the active canvas/tool path still constructs and executes commands directly: `SvgCanvas.vue` imports command/operation classes and pushes commands for point drag and style edits, tool classes receive `world` plus `commandStack`, `ArgumentCollectorTool` owns an open-command lifecycle, and concrete tools import factories and process-tree operations. The same scope check found that `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` are real but separate command-construction surfaces with distinct controller concepts and validation surfaces, requiring the 006a/006b split before implementation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:251:The active canvas/tool path has Vue components and tool interaction classes emit typed user intents, collected arguments, and interaction state while application use-cases/tool controllers own command construction, validation orchestration, command-stack execution, commit/rollback, feasibility checks, undo/redo lifecycle, point movement, and style update for canvas-driven construction. Tool/factory modules touched by that path have a coherent application-owned location and dependency direction. `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` remain known, intentional residuals for Wave 006b rather than accidental leftovers.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:255:- Delete direct imports of `Command`, `CommandStack`, and concrete operations from `SvgCanvas.vue` for the canvas/tool path addressed by this wave, including point movement and style updates.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:256:- Delete `Command`/`CommandStack` exposure from `ToolContext` and any active open-command tool execution APIs.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:269:- Existing e2e construction, guide, intersection, dynamic-plane, selection, properties/style, point-move, and undo/redo flows pass as part of the mandatory validation cadence.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:285:- example/debug construction setup use-cases or deletion decisions for toolbar shortcuts such as Alt+E
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:291:This wave begins only after Wave 006a completes and its implementation log records any remaining panel/toolbar command leaks. It must not reopen the canvas/tool controller extraction except for consuming the stable application command/use-case boundary created in 006a. If the toolbar example construction is judged debug-only, prefer deletion over migrating a dead shortcut.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:295:The baseline flags direct UI-to-command/operation imports as an architectural leak (`architecture-map.md` leak 3; `risk-register.md` R5; `quality-report.md` Q1, Q4, Q19). The Wave 006 scope check found that `ActionsPanel.vue` directly imports `Command` plus concrete process-tree operations and constructs/pushes commands in panel handlers, while `Toolbar.vue` directly imports `Command`, `CommandStack`, `Vector3`, and concrete world operations for the Alt+E example setup. The scope check also concluded these panel/toolbar paths are separate from canvas tools because they require process-history edit use-cases, debug/example construction decisions, and independent validation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:299:Panel and toolbar components emit typed user intents and render state only. Application use-cases own process-tree stack/action mutations, command creation, validation, and command-stack execution for panel actions. Toolbar example/setup behavior is either deleted as debug-only or routed through an explicit application construction/example use-case without direct concrete operation imports in the component.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:305:- Delete `Toolbar.vue` imports of `Command`, `CommandStack`, `Vector3`, `AddFreePoint2DOp`, `AddLine2DByTwoPointsOp`, and any other concrete command/operation construction dependencies used only by the Alt+E/example setup path.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:306:- Delete the Toolbar Alt+E example/debug shortcut entirely if review/implementation determines it is not user-facing architecture worth preserving.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:307:- Delete panel/toolbar tests that inspect command internals or rely on debug/example construction details; replace them with use-case/controller tests and behavior/e2e coverage for retained flows.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:316:- Mandatory validation cadence, relevant e2e flows, and live preview refresh/probe pass.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:413:The baseline classifies `App.vue` as a god shell that creates `World`, `CommandStack`, Drive service, local project state, autosave timers, keyboard/delete/select-all behavior, active-plane state, project lifecycle, and command coordination (`quality-report.md` Q4; `architecture-map.md` “Entry points and workspace boundaries”; `risk-register.md` R2, R5).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:423:- Delete debug/error TODO stubs that are not implemented by the extracted services.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:437:## Wave 010 — Resolve backend/frontend project-contract architecture
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:441:- `backend/src/models/Project.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:442:- `backend/src/api/projects.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:443:- `backend/src/storage/**`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:444:- `backend/tests/**`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:445:- frontend direct backend import in `frontend/src/components/dialogs/OpenProjectDialog.vue`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:446:- root and workspace package scripts if backend remains active
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:451:The baseline finds the backend is a parallel/disconnected Express/file-storage path with broad `any` DTOs, root scripts skip backend validation, and the frontend imports `ProjectMetadata` directly from backend source while using frontend `ProjectService`/Drive rather than backend routes (`architecture-map.md` backend sections and corrections; `quality-report.md` Q9, Q18, Q20, Q22; `risk-register.md` R9, R12).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:455:The project has one explicit backend stance: either delete the unused backend/project API from the active architecture, or make it a real validated boundary with a shared typed contract and root validation. Do not preserve a disconnected backend plus frontend source import leak.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:459:- Delete the direct frontend import from `backend/src/models/Project` regardless of chosen backend stance.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:460:- If backend is unused, delete backend routes/models/storage/tests and workspace references instead of preserving a stale server.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:461:- If backend remains active, delete broad backend DTO `any` fields in favor of shared contract types and add backend to root validation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:462:- Delete docs/spec references that describe a backend project API no longer present.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:466:- No frontend code imports backend implementation files by relative monorepo path.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:472:Depends on Wave 004 because backend project DTOs must align with the chosen frontend persistence contract, and on Wave 009 if app shell persistence routing is moved first.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:476:## Wave 011 — Tighten architecture tests and root validation gates
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:483:- `backend/package.json` if backend remains active
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:485:- CI/local validation documentation only where needed for test commands
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:489:The baseline says existing architecture tests cover narrow rules (no command subclasses and serialization coverage) while allowing broader violations: domain-to-command/service imports, UI-to-operation imports, and duplicate store/domain ownership (`quality-report.md` Q19; `risk-register.md` R10). It also reports root scripts skip backend despite workspaces declaring frontend and backend (`quality-report.md` Q18; `risk-register.md` R9).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:493:The cleaned boundaries from previous waves are enforced by tests and package scripts so architectural erosion is caught early. Root validation reflects the actual active workspaces.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:503:- Architecture tests fail on domain-to-command/service imports, UI-to-operation imports, duplicate geometry state ownership, and forbidden frontend→backend implementation imports according to the boundaries established by earlier waves.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:505:- Full validation cadence passes.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:513:## Wave 012 — Remove vacant placeholders, noisy debug surfaces, and obsolete operation docs
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:519:- debug-only operation paths such as `frontend/src/commands/operations/debug/` if no longer active
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:520:- debug logging in `frontend/src/App.vue`, `frontend/src/services/ProjectService.ts`, `frontend/src/services/PaperService.ts`, `frontend/src/services/GoogleDriveService.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:526:Corrections identify `paper/` and `toolbar/` operation directories as README-only future-operation placeholders and deletion candidates (`inventory.md` corrections; `quality-report.md` Q21; `risk-register.md` R13). The baseline also flags TODOs in user-facing rendering/feedback paths and debug logging across app/service code (`quality-report.md` Q15, Q17).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:530:The active source tree contains no aspirational placeholder operation families, no obsolete TODO branches from refactored paths, and no routine debug logging that obscures boundaries or risks leaking integration metadata.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:536:- Delete debug operation directories/files if not used by validated current behavior.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:537:- Delete routine console debug logging and obsolete TODO stubs in touched modules.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:542:- No debug logging remains in normal app/persistence/rendering/Drive paths except explicitly justified error logging.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:543:- Tests and e2e confirm no deleted debug/example path was required for normal behavior.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:547:Depends on Waves 004, 006, 007, and 009 so placeholder/delete decisions are made after command, rendering, and shell boundaries are settled.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:564:The baseline inventories the current `specs/001-dihedral-learning-mvp/` tree and the docs areas under `docs/architecture/`, `docs/api/`, `docs/guide/`, and `docs/contributing/testing.md` (`inventory.md` Specs and Docs sections). It also notes that current code comments still cite the original MVP spec for world/process/command concepts and that README-only operation placeholders are deletion candidates (`inventory.md` corrections; `quality-report.md` Q21; `risk-register.md` R13). Earlier objectives require docs/comments waves after central domain, persistence, rendering, tool-state, and UI composition work.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:572:- Delete docs pages that describe removed legacy state paths, command subclass assumptions, placeholder operation families, or disconnected backend behavior.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:578:- Docs match the cleaned module boundaries and validation commands.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:579:- No documentation encourages use of deleted APIs, legacy stores, command subclasses, migration shims, or backend paths that no longer exist.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:588:## Wave 014 — Establish validation contract ownership and delete invalid gates
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:594:  - Wave 013 validation failures and logs (`architecture-audit/cycle-013-docs-specs-reconciliation/validation/final-validation-summary.stdout`)
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:595:- Contract-owned repair/delete decisions for the currently red validation surfaces:
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:601:- Narrow architecture-test or validation-health assertions only if they prevent recurrence of corrupted specs, zero-test files, or unsupported build-tool/runtime combinations evidenced by the current red gates.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:605:This wave owns validation contracts, not product redesign. It may create small validation-health checks, but it must not add a large custom test runner, broad allowlist framework, generalized validation platform, parallel build/test toolchain, or sweeping cleanup of unrelated test debt. Recurrence checks are allowed only for the exact invalid artifact classes evidenced by the current red gates: corrupted Playwright parse artifacts, zero-test/placeholder integration files, and unsupported build-tool/runtime combinations. If a red surface has no current product/architecture owner, delete the stale test and replace it with a smaller current-owner test rather than preserving historical coverage theater.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:607:Intersection ownership is deliberately bounded. Implementation must first enumerate every active caller and test assumption for the line-line intersection API, then record a selected contract statement choosing one result model for non-intersection cases: nullable result, infeasible realization, or discriminated result. Only source/tests directly affected by that selected contract may change in Wave 014. If that caller inventory proves the right fix is a deeper geometry/domain-model redesign, Wave 014 implementation must stop and trigger a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle; the redesign must not be hidden under validation contract ownership.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:617:- Integration tests contain executable behavior, not placeholder comments.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:621:The result should still be green root/frontend Vitest, build, and e2e, but the primary outcome is a cleaned validation architecture that future waves can trust. Validation contract ownership remains distinct from central domain correctness, tooling compatibility, and stale/corrupted test artifacts: this wave may align those contracts where they block mandatory gates, but it may not convert them into a broad validation framework or unrelated source refactor.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:626:- Delete zero-test/placeholder-only integration cases from `guide-creation.test.ts`; convert only current behavior into real executable tests.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:634:- A short validation-ownership note in the implementation log classifies each former red surface as domain, UI component, integration, e2e, or tooling ownership and cites the resolved file(s).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:640:- Live preview is refreshed on `0.0.0.0:5173` and returns HTTP 200, with evidence captured under the implementation cycle validation logs.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:641:- Any new architecture/static check is narrow and prevents recurrence of the exact class of invalid validation artifact, not a broad maintenance framework.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:654:- This framing can overreach if it attempts to redesign the full validation system. Keep it bounded to known red gates and recurrence checks for exact invalid artifact classes only.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:662:Depends on Waves 001–013 and should precede any subsequent source refactor wave, because it establishes trustworthy validation ownership for future changes.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:670:This wave is bounded to the exact active TypeScript build backlog produced by `architecture-audit/cycle-014-validation-contract-ownership/validation/frontend-build.after5.stdout` and only the direct type/contract-owner dependencies required to eliminate those exact errors honestly. It may touch source/tests/package metadata only where a current build error or its direct owner contract requires convergence of the following boundaries; nearby files, adjacent cleanup, naming/formatting improvements, and unrelated architecture normalization are out of scope:
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:688:   - `frontend/src/domain/world/World.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:711:The result is a trustworthy TypeScript/build gate that future architecture waves and the deferred Wave 014 validation closure can rely on.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:720:- stale `JsonValue | undefined` descriptor plumbing where a typed descriptor or validation guard is the correct boundary.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:721:- stale service singleton imports such as `projectService` where only `ProjectService` is exported.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:769:All stdout/stderr must be captured under the Wave 015 validation directory. If any command is undefined at root, record exact package-script evidence instead of silently skipping.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:781:Depends on Wave 014 implementation commits `1b1f37cbbb8fb8da96645c40160fce0384a5b316` and `806e1ac754899d0ad6d0322afd6b4a35a77d0f12`, because those scoped repairs and the `vue-tsc` update reveal the real build backlog. Must complete before `cycle-014-validation-and-preview-refresh`, because Wave 014 success criteria require root/frontend build success and live preview refresh.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:785:After Wave 015 completes, do not proceed to an unrelated refactor wave. Re-enter `cycle-014-validation-and-preview-refresh` and run the full Wave 014 validation cadence with stable logs against the source tree containing both Wave 014 scoped validation repairs and Wave 015 build/type-contract repairs: root/frontend lint where defined, focused and full Vitest, root/frontend build, root/frontend e2e/Playwright, live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173` with HTTP reachability probe, and Playwright MCP/live checks for the repaired keyboard-shortcut and selection-mask flows. Close Wave 014 only if those mandatory gates pass.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:799:- a new narrow canvas presentation read-port module such as `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:810:This wave replaces the now-invalid old Wave 007/008/009 premises rather than continuing them literally. The old Wave 007 named `PaperService.ts` and `RenderService.ts`, but current scout evidence says those files are absent and the active rendering path is `SVGRenderer.ts` plus `canvasPresentation/*`. The old Wave 008 assumed a large `ThreeView.vue` extraction, but current `ThreeView.vue` is already a small runtime shell. The old Wave 009 app-shell god-component premise is also mostly invalidated by the current `App.vue` runtime/action composition. Therefore this wave is the next central post-Wave-015 source boundary: active canvas presentation still receives full `World`, contains `any` seams, embeds projected-3D-on-2D ownership, and has user-observable overlay pointer interception that validation previously routed around.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:812:Do not reopen broad rendering, 3D scene, app-shell, command, persistence, backend, package/tooling, docs/spec, or validation-platform redesigns inside this wave. If implementation discovers those areas are required, stop and run a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle before broadening scope.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:816:The 2D canvas presentation path consumes explicit narrow read ports/DTOs instead of a full mutable `World`; 2D primitive selection, projected-3D-on-2D projection, hit/query input, style policy, visibility policy, and overlay event ownership have named modules with one responsibility each. Broad `any` helper seams in active presentation code are deleted rather than cast over. The 3D overlay either deliberately owns pointer interaction in its region or deliberately allows underlying canvas interaction; that contract is encoded in source and tests instead of hidden in e2e click-coordinate workarounds.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:820:- Delete `CanvasPresentationController.buildModel(world: World, ...)` or any equivalent full-`World` selector API once the reviewed read port is introduced.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:823:- Delete obsolete selector tests that instantiate concrete `World` only to inspect traversal internals; replace them with read-port/DTO tests.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:826:- Delete any compatibility wrapper, dual full-`World`/read-port path, broad presentation facade, or dead export created during the transition.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:830:- Canvas presentation selectors/controllers no longer depend on a full mutable `World` where a narrow read port/DTO is sufficient.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:834:- No broad rendering platform, shared god snapshot factory, app-shell redesign, command/persistence/backend change, package/tooling change, or stale audit-history dependency is introduced.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:835:- Mandatory validation cadence passes with stable logs: root/frontend lint where defined, focused Vitest for touched presentation/query modules, full root/frontend Vitest, root/frontend build, root/frontend e2e when user-facing overlay/canvas behavior changes, live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173`, HTTP reachability probe, and Playwright/live browser evidence for the affected canvas/overlay region when applicable.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:839:Depends on Wave 015 and the subsequent Wave 014 validation closure, because this wave relies on the now-green TypeScript/build/test/e2e gates and on the extracted command/use-case, rendering, and validation contracts produced by earlier cycles. It is ordered before any remaining peripheral docs/debug cleanup because it is the most central current user-input/rendering boundary left after Wave 015.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:843:1. Capture static scans for full-`World` canvas presentation APIs, `CanvasPresentationSelector` `any` seams, projected-3D loops, `Paper` query usage, and current 3D overlay pointer handlers before edits.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:878:- `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts` only where needed to align realization/read-context ownership and avoid duplicate presentation adapters
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:879:- `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasVisibilityPolicy.ts` only if the reviewed implementation can delete its `world as World` cast through a narrow visibility/process-tree capability without broad style/process redesign
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:886:This wave continues Wave 016's active presentation-boundary cleanup. It must not reopen broad rendering, scene-host/OrbitControls, app-shell, command/undo, persistence, backend, docs/spec, package/tooling, validation-platform, or Drive/local project architecture. It may touch canvas presentation only to share or align the read-port/realization context; it must not rewrite the Wave 016 selector model or introduce a global presentation snapshot god-module. If implementation proves that domain realization itself needs a larger redesign, stop for a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle rather than hiding it in this wave.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:890:Wave 016 completed the 2D canvas presentation read-port/overlay boundary and recorded a residual risk that `CanvasWorldReadPort` still delegates realization calls through concrete runtime `World` (`architecture-audit/cycle-016-canvas-presentation-overlay-boundary/implementation-log.md`, “Residual risks / future candidates”). Current source shows `CanvasWorldReadPort` exposes 2D/3D realization capabilities and the adapter calls entity realization with `world` (`frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:14`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:21`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:24`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:46`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:49`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:50`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:51`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:892:The active 3D presentation path still accepts full mutable `World`: `ThreePresentationInput` embeds `world: World` (`frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationModel.ts:1`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationModel.ts:10`), `ThreePresentationSelector` passes it to every concern selector (`frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:17`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:20`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:21`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:22`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:23`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreePresentationSelector.ts:24`), and `ThreeView.vue` still injects `World` and passes it into the runtime refresh input (`frontend/src/components/panels/ThreeView.vue:29`, `frontend/src/components/panels/ThreeView.vue:41`, `frontend/src/components/panels/ThreeView.vue:46`). Existing domain projection realization context interfaces provide a possible lower-level read-capability vocabulary (`frontend/src/domain/world/projectionPlane/ProjectionPlaneContext.ts:9`, `frontend/src/domain/world/projectionPlane/ProjectionPlaneContext.ts:20`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:896:3D presentation selectors/controllers consume explicit read ports or read-context slices instead of full mutable `World`. Canvas and 3D presentation do not maintain contradictory realization/read adapter models: either a small 3D read port mirrors the successful Wave 016 boundary without duplicating broad world access, or a reviewed shared presentation read-context replaces duplicate adapters. Internal full-`World` selector APIs and touched test `any` workarounds are deleted, not wrapped. User-visible 2D and 3D rendering remains equivalent.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:900:- Delete `ThreePresentationInput.world: World` as the active 3D presentation input contract.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:901:- Delete full-`World` selector signatures in active three-presentation selectors where a read port/context slice is sufficient.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:904:- Delete the `CanvasVisibilityPolicy` `world as World` cast only if replaced by a narrow typed visibility/process-tree capability inside the reviewed scope.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:905:- Delete transitional overloads, compatibility wrappers, and dead exports created while moving from full `World` to read ports.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:909:- `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/*` no longer exposes a public application-presentation input or selector API requiring full mutable `World` where a read port/context slice is sufficient.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:910:- `ThreeView.vue` remains a composition shell and wraps the injected runtime `World` only at the boundary needed to create the selected read port/context.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:911:- Canvas and 3D presentation realization/read access are aligned by a documented boundary; no parallel full-World and read-port paths remain for the same selector responsibility.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:913:- No scene-host/OrbitControls redesign, app-shell redesign, command/persistence/backend change, package/tooling change, or docs/spec rewrite is introduced.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:914:- Mandatory validation cadence passes with stable logs: root/frontend lint where defined, focused Vitest for touched presentation/read-port modules, full root/frontend Vitest, root/frontend build, root/frontend e2e, live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173`, HTTP reachability probe, and Playwright/live browser evidence for 3D view plus representative 2D canvas rendering.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:922:1. Capture pre-edit static scans for full-`World` APIs/imports in three presentation, `CanvasWorldReadPort` realization delegation, `CanvasVisibilityPolicy` casts, and touched-test `as any` seams.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:932:- A read port can become a renamed `World` facade if it mirrors too many methods. Keep capabilities tied to selector responsibilities.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:950:- 3D realization classes that still type realization against concrete `World` or `any`:
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:964:- Presentation read-port adapters that currently pass concrete `World` into domain realization:
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:965:  - `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:966:  - `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:971:This wave owns the domain realization context contract, not a broad rendering, scene-host, command, persistence, app-shell, backend, validation-platform, docs/spec, or package/tooling redesign. It must begin with a current caller inventory for `realize2D(...)`, `realize3D(...)`, `getPlaneEquation(...)`, and projection-plane realization helper APIs, then choose the smallest typed context boundary that removes concrete `World`/`any` from active realization contracts without introducing a new god context.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:977:The baseline already identified domain-internal realization coupling as a residual central risk: `Element2D` required realization through a whole `World`, `styleService` walked `World`/`ProcessTree`, and weak realization contexts used `any` (`architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R6 and R11; `architecture-audit/baseline/architecture-map.md`, “Additional service/domain coupling paths”). Waves 016 and 017 deliberately narrowed canvas and 3D presentation selectors to read ports, but both closure logs record the same residual: presentation read-port adapters still call domain realization APIs with concrete runtime `World` (`architecture-audit/cycle-016-canvas-presentation-overlay-boundary/implementation-log.md`, “Residual risks / future candidates”; `architecture-audit/cycle-017-three-presentation-read-port-realization-boundary/implementation-log.md`, “Residual risks after closure”).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:979:Current source evidence confirms that the residual is now a domain-realization boundary, not a selector-boundary problem. `CanvasWorldReadPort` exposes realization methods and its adapter calls `plane.realizePoint2D(point, world)`, `plane.realizeLine2D(line, world)`, `plane.realizeCircle2D(circle, world)`, `point.realize3D(world)`, `line.realize3D(world)`, and `segment.realize3D(world)` (`frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:14`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:21`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:30`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:46`, `frontend/src/application/canvasPresentation/CanvasWorldReadPort.ts:51`). `ThreeWorldReadPort` similarly wraps `World` and builds read models by calling `point.realize2D(world)`, `line.realize2D(world)`, `circle.realize2D(world)`, selected-guide `line.realize2D(world)`, and hinge `realize3D(world)` inside the adapter (`frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts:55`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts:65`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts:84`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts:98`, `frontend/src/application/threePresentation/ThreeWorldReadPort.ts:117`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:981:The domain has already started the right vocabulary for 2D realization with `ProjectionElementRealizationContext` (`frontend/src/domain/world/projectionPlane/ProjectionPlaneContext.ts:9`, `frontend/src/domain/world/projectionPlane/ProjectionPlaneContext.ts:20`), and `Element2D.realize2D(context: ProjectionElementRealizationContext)` now uses that type (`frontend/src/domain/world/Element2D.ts:1`, `frontend/src/domain/world/Element2D.ts:108`). The remaining central weakness is that 3D realization and plane-equation contracts still use concrete `World` or `any`: `Element3D.realize3D(world: any): any` (`frontend/src/domain/world/Element3D.ts:40`), `Point3D`/`Line3D`/`Segment3D` import `World` for realization (`frontend/src/domain/world/Point3D.ts`, `frontend/src/domain/world/Line3D.ts`, `frontend/src/domain/world/Segment3D.ts`), and `Plane` uses `any` in plane-equation/realization APIs (`frontend/src/domain/world/Plane.ts`). Static scans in `architecture-audit/cycle-018-wave-plan-extension/static-realization-world-scan.stdout` and `static-any-scan.stdout` capture the current residual seams.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:985:Domain realization APIs consume explicit typed realization/read contexts rather than full mutable `World` or broad `any`. 2D and 3D realization share a coherent context vocabulary where their lookup needs overlap, with separate narrow capabilities where they do not. Canvas and three presentation read-port adapters may still be the runtime boundary that wraps a concrete `World`, but they pass only the selected realization context into domain realization APIs. The active source no longer has a parallel situation where selectors use read ports while domain realization contracts force adapters back to full `World`/`any`.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:991:- Delete concrete `World` realization signatures from active 3D domain elements where a typed context is sufficient, including point/line/segment/plane/hinge realization paths covered by the caller inventory.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:992:- Delete presentation adapter calls that pass full `World` directly to `realize2D(...)`, `realize3D(...)`, or plane-equation APIs when the new context is available.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:995:- Delete transitional overloads, compatibility wrappers, dead exports, and type aliases that preserve both old full-`World` and new context APIs.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1000:- Active 3D realization base contracts no longer use `any` or concrete `World` where a typed realization context is sufficient.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1002:- `CanvasWorldReadPort` and `ThreeWorldReadPort` pass typed realization contexts into domain realization calls rather than exposing selector-side read ports that internally depend on full-`World` realization signatures.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1004:- No source change is made to command/undo, persistence/project schema, app-shell orchestration, backend contracts, package/tooling, docs/specs, Drive/local services, or broad rendering/scene-host modules except if a reviewed mini-cycle explicitly broadens scope.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1005:- Mandatory validation cadence passes with stable logs: root/frontend lint where defined, focused Vitest for realization/read-port tests, full root/frontend Vitest, root/frontend build, root/frontend e2e because canvas/3D rendering is user-observable, live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173`, HTTP reachability probe, and Playwright/live smoke for representative 2D canvas plus 3D view rendering.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1009:Depends on Waves 016 and 017 because those waves moved presentation selectors behind canvas/three read ports and exposed the remaining full-`World` seam as a domain realization issue. It is ordered before broader 3D scene-host lifecycle, app-shell service concentration, backend/frontend contract stance, validation-gate hardening beyond existing gates, placeholder/debug cleanup, and docs/spec drift because those later topics should consume a stable domain realization/read-context contract rather than preserve the current full-`World` realization API.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1013:1. Capture pre-edit static scans for `realize2D`, `realize3D`, `getPlaneEquation`, `World` imports in realization classes, `any` realization contracts, and read-port adapter calls that pass full `World` into realization.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1023:- A single “realization context” can become a renamed `World` facade if it exposes every lookup/mutation method. Keep capabilities read-only and scoped to actual realization caller needs from the inventory.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1024:- Changing realization signatures can ripple widely through geometry construction and rendering. Use typed context builders and focused tests before full validation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1026:- Some domain comments still reference old phase-era `World` examples. Do not convert this into a docs/comment cleanup wave; update only comments adjacent to changed contracts if needed for correctness.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1031:The old full-`World`/`any` realization signatures are architectural debt, not a compatibility surface worth preserving. Keeping overloads or aliases would leave the core domain contract ambiguous and would let future presentation adapters continue bypassing narrow read contexts. Under the project priorities, delete the old signatures and update active callers/tests directly once the reviewed proposal chooses the exact context shape.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1058:This wave owns the Three.js runtime/platform boundary after the presentation and domain-realization read contracts are stable. It must not reopen domain realization contracts, canvas presentation selectors, app-shell project lifecycle, command/undo, persistence, backend, package/tooling, validation-platform, docs/spec, or broad UI redesign. It must begin with a current inventory of scene-host responsibilities, animation-frame ownership, resize/event ownership, renderer/label-renderer DOM ownership, OrbitControls ownership, registry disposal, and active scene-adapter material/geometry creation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1060:If Wave 018 leaves full-`World` or `any` realization seams that still force renderer decisions to depend on domain internals, stop and run a reviewed plan edit before starting this wave. If implementation discovers user-facing 3D interaction behavior must change beyond encoding the existing lifecycle/ownership contract, cover it with focused e2e/live evidence or stop for a mini-cycle.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1064:The baseline identified 3D rendering concentration as a major residual rendering risk: `ThreeView.vue` originally combined scene setup, projection-plane rendering, 2D-on-3D rendering, 3D elements, guide geometry, hinge lines, animation, resize, cleanup, domain realization access, and store access (`architecture-audit/baseline/quality-report.md`, Q5; `architecture-audit/baseline/architecture-map.md`, “God-modules”; `architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R8). Waves 016 and 017 narrowed the canvas/three presentation read-model boundary first, and Wave 018 is ordered ahead of this entry so the scene platform consumes typed realization/read outputs rather than preserving full-`World` realization seams.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1087:- No domain realization, canvas presentation, command/undo, persistence, backend, app-shell project lifecycle, package/tooling, validation-platform, or docs/spec changes are introduced without a reviewed plan edit.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1088:- Mandatory validation cadence passes with stable logs: root/frontend lint where defined, focused Vitest for three-scene-host/runtime/registry/adapter modules, full root/frontend Vitest, root/frontend build, root/frontend Playwright e2e because 3D rendering is user-observable, live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173`, HTTP reachability probe, and Playwright/live browser evidence for representative 3D rendering and controls.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1092:Depends on Wave 018 because scene-host lifecycle should consume stable typed realization/read-context outputs rather than compensate for domain realization contracts that still require full `World` or `any`. It is ordered before app-shell service concentration, backend/frontend contract stance, validation-gate hardening, placeholder/debug cleanup, and docs/spec drift because the remaining 3D runtime/platform boundary is still central user-visible architecture while those later topics either depend on stable rendering contracts or are more peripheral cleanup.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1130:- `frontend/src/application/appShell/SelectionUseCases.ts` only where selection/delete command ownership must be kept behind the app-shell/application boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1135:- `frontend/src/services/ProjectService.ts` only where its app-shell-facing persistence port shape must be narrowed or moved behind a typed adapter
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1141:This wave owns the app-shell project/session/browser integration boundary after the domain, command, validation, presentation, realization, and 3D runtime boundaries have been stabilized. It must not reopen domain realization, canvas/three presentation selectors, Three.js scene-host lifecycle, command/undo descriptor architecture, project serialization internals, backend deletion/contract stance, package/tooling gates, docs/spec reconciliation, or broad store redesign. If implementation proves that persistence format or command serialization must be redesigned rather than merely hidden behind a stable app-shell port, stop and run a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle before broadening scope.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1143:The wave must start with a current inventory of concrete construction and browser globals in the app shell: `World`, `CommandStack`, `GoogleDriveService`, `ProjectService`, `window.localStorage`, keyboard target installation, scheduler/timer installation, alert callbacks, auth/session storage keys, and runtime state exposed to `App.vue`. It must also inventory which app-shell use cases still directly mutate `World`/`CommandStack` and which of those are intentional application-use-case dependencies versus composition leaks.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1147:The original baseline ranked `App.vue` as an app-shell god module that owned `World`, `CommandStack`, Drive/local project state, autosave, keyboard/delete/select-all behavior, active-plane state, project lifecycle, and command coordination (`architecture-audit/baseline/architecture-map.md`, “Entry points and workspace boundaries”; `architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R2 and R5). Earlier waves have reduced the Vue shell itself: current `App.vue` delegates startup, toolbar, project-name, canvas, and lifecycle events to `runtime.actions`, creates the runtime through `createAppShellRuntime`, and only provides `runtime.world` plus `runtime.commandStack` to descendants (`frontend/src/App.vue:12`, `frontend/src/App.vue:24`, `frontend/src/App.vue:63`, `frontend/src/App.vue:107`, `frontend/src/App.vue:126`, `frontend/src/App.vue:142`, `frontend/src/App.vue:143`, `frontend/src/App.vue:171`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1149:Current source evidence shows the remaining central problem moved into the application app-shell runtime/composition boundary, not into `App.vue` markup. `createAppShellRuntime` still constructs `World`, `CommandStack`, default `GoogleDriveService`, default `ProjectService`, browser `window.localStorage`, browser scheduler, and installs keyboard handling on `window` from the runtime (`frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:21`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:22`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:23`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:24`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:27`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:28`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:29`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts:101`). `AppShellPorts` still exposes a persistence port typed directly in terms of `CommandStack`, `World`, and `ProjectData` from `ProjectService`, so app-session orchestration is not insulated from concrete command/domain/persistence shapes (`frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:2`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:3`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:5`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:24`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:25`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellPorts.ts:27`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1151:Project/session use cases still mix session state, Drive calls, local-storage keys, domain reset/initialization, command-stack clearing, persistence validation/deserialization, autosave start, and error UI state in one class (`frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:27`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:40`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:47`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:96`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:99`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:102`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:118`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:121`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:123`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:124`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/ProjectSessionUseCases.ts:188`). `AutoSaveUseCases` owns dirty-version polling, debounce/status timers, persistence serialization, Drive update/create fallback, local-storage last-file update, auth-expiry UI behavior, and quota/file-deleted error policy (`frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:30`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:33`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:42`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:50`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:54`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:59`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:92`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:103`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AutoSaveUseCases.ts:112`).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1153:Recent Wave 019 closure confirms validation is green and the current plan ends at Wave 019, so a new central wave is required before implementation continues (`architecture-audit/cycle-019-three-scene-host-lifecycle-renderer-platform/implementation-log.md`, “Validation/preview closure — cycle-019-validation-and-preview-refresh”). Current scans also show lower-priority candidates are not more central right now: the backend directory is absent and root workspaces list only `frontend` (`package.json:7`, `package.json:8`, `package.json:13`, `.saivage/stages/cycle-020-wave-plan-extension/backend-contract-source-evidence.stdout`); README-only `paper/`/`toolbar/` operation placeholder directories were not found (`.saivage/stages/cycle-020-wave-plan-extension/deferred-candidates-evidence.stdout`); docs/spec drift remains real but intentionally follows structural waves.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1159:The persistence-facing port should express the app shell's need to save/load a current project session without forcing use-case code to know static `ProjectService` methods or serialization internals. The Drive-facing port should remain a Drive/project-file adapter contract, not a direct dependency on Google API globals. Browser state keys such as `diedrico_last_file_id` and `diedrico_last_file_name` should be centralized behind a small session-storage port or repository instead of duplicated string literals in project/session code.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1163:- Delete default construction of `GoogleDriveService`, direct `ProjectService` static binding, direct `window.localStorage`, and direct `window` keyboard target installation from `createAppShellRuntime` if the reviewed proposal introduces a production composition factory/adapters.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1164:- Delete `ProjectPersistencePort` methods that expose concrete static `ProjectService` shape to app-session use cases if replaced by a narrower project-session persistence adapter.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1169:- Do not delete or rewrite `GoogleDriveService` internals, `ProjectService` serialization internals, command descriptors, or backend/docs/spec files in this wave unless a reviewed plan edit broadens scope.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1173:- `createAppShellRuntime` can be constructed in tests and in production from explicit dependencies without directly reaching for `window.localStorage`, default `GoogleDriveService`, or static `ProjectService` as hidden globals inside the runtime core.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1177:- Selection/delete and keyboard routing behavior remains user-visible equivalent; any retained direct `World`/`CommandStack` use in app-shell use cases is documented as an intentional application-use-case dependency rather than a composition leak.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1178:- No domain realization, rendering/presentation, Three.js scene-host, command/undo descriptor, persistence schema, backend, docs/spec, package/tooling, or validation-platform change is introduced without a reviewed plan edit.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1180:- Mandatory validation cadence passes with stable logs captured under the Wave 020 `validation/` directory: `npm run lint` at the root and in every workspace where defined; focused app-shell/service-adapter Vitest for touched modules; full Vitest at the root and in every workspace where defined; `npm run build` at the root and in every workspace where defined; Playwright/e2e at the root and in every workspace where defined because project/session and keyboard flows are user-observable; live preview refresh on `0.0.0.0:5173`; HTTP reachability probe; and Playwright MCP/live browser evidence for startup/new project, keyboard routing, and save/autosave-visible status where practical.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1184:Depends on Waves 014–019 because it should consume green validation gates, stable command/use-case contracts, current project persistence contracts, cleaned presentation/read-port contracts, typed realization contexts, and the explicit Three scene-host lifecycle boundary. It is ordered before backend/frontend contract stance, placeholder/debug cleanup, docs/spec reconciliation, and broad validation hardening because current evidence shows the active frontend app-shell project/session boundary is the next central runtime coordinator while the backend is absent from the active workspace and docs/debug cleanup would be peripheral.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1188:All Wave 020 validation stdout/stderr must be captured to stable files under `architecture-audit/cycle-020-app-shell-project-session-browser-integration/validation/` (or the reviewed final Cycle 020 directory name). Undefined commands may be skipped only with exact package-script evidence in the implementation log; defined root or workspace commands are mandatory.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1190:1. Capture pre-edit scoped git status/diff and static scans for hidden app-shell concrete construction/browser globals, project-session storage-key duplication, app-shell persistence/Drive port shapes, keyboard target installation, and direct `World`/`CommandStack` mutation in app-shell use cases.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1191:2. Run focused Vitest for changed app-shell runtime/use cases/ports/adapters and any touched ProjectService/GoogleDriveService adapter tests, with stable stdout/stderr logs.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1203:- Drive/auth behavior cannot rely on real credentials in validation. Use fake-port unit coverage and credential-free browser smoke; do not read env/secret/config files.
architecture-audit/baseline/corrections-round-01.md:9:| Reviewer-A C1; Reviewer-B C1 — inventory missing modules | Addressed | `inventory.md` | Added domain-world support files and descriptor registry (`frontend/src/domain/world/Element.ts:29`, `frontend/src/domain/world/Element2D.ts:1`, `frontend/src/domain/world/Realization.ts:37`, `frontend/src/domain/world/descriptors/DescriptorRegistry.ts:15`), services (`frontend/src/services/GeometryService.ts:21`, `frontend/src/services/InputService.ts:17`, `frontend/src/services/ValidationService.ts:16`), types (`frontend/src/types/ResultTypes.ts:2`, `frontend/src/types/ToolArgumentCollector.ts:17`), operation subdirectories including README-only placeholders (`frontend/src/commands/operations/paper/README.md:7`, `frontend/src/commands/operations/toolbar/README.md:7`), and test breadth. |
